{"id":4402,"date":"2021-12-06T15:21:43","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:21:43","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4402"},"modified":"2024-12-02T17:30:30","modified_gmt":"2024-12-03T01:30:30","slug":"2-5-random-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-5-random-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"2.5: Random"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af09a9d\" name=\"6a2213af09a9d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af09c00\" name=\"6a2213af09c00\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe how a computer generates random numbers<\/li>\n<li>Describe the range of numbers generated by default from a random number generator<\/li>\n<li>Explain how to convert random numbers into useable ranges of values in a program<\/li>\n<li>Explain how random numbers can be valuable in a program<\/li>\n<li>Write a program that uses random numbers to impact drone flight<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Random Function<\/li>\n<li>Random LED<\/li>\n<li>Random Practice Exercises<\/li>\n<li>Random Flight Path<\/li>\n<li>Number Guessing Program<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Magic 8 Ball<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Random Flight<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the ways you can see return values with your CoDrone EDU is through its LED and number randomization. Quick review from the LED lesson: <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">an LED produces color as a combination of red, green, and blue. All of these values range from 0 to 255 corresponding to the color&#8217;s intensity, with 255 being the highest.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this program, import the random library. Next, create a for loop that will run three times.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In the loop, create variables that will get a random number between 0 and 255 for red, blue, and green. Those three numbers are combined to change the LED\u2019s color. For example, if your program returned RGB values of 13, 246, and 197, your CoDrone EDU\u2019s LED would turn aqua.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, have your drone take off, wait briefly, and then return the red, green, and blue random numbers with a brightness of 100. This should change the color of your drone\u2019s LED. After that, have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try this program out and see what happens. Once you get it working, try to add a random value for brightness as well!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\nimport random\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(3):\r\n\r\n    red = random.randint(0,255)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 blue = random.randint(0,255)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 green = random.randint(0,255)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_drone_LED(red,blue,green, 100)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af09c65\" name=\"6a2213af09c65\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random Function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The\u00a0<em><strong>random<\/strong><\/em><em>\u00a0<\/em>library in Python allows use to generate a variety of random numbers. To learn more about the random library and its functions, click <a href=\"https:\/\/docs.python.org\/3\/library\/random.html\">HERE<\/a> to go to the official documentation. In the following video, we explore how random numbers work in Python. Make sure you create a new file called <em>random_practice<\/em> before getting started:<\/p>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/0FwOgiAy_0E[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af09dea\" name=\"6a2213af09dea\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random LED<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]One of the ways you can see return values with your CoDrone EDU is through its LED and number randomization. Quick review from the LED lesson: an LED produces color as a combination of red, green, and blue. All of these values range from 0 to 255 corresponding to the color\u2019s intensity, with 255 being the highest.<\/p>\n<p>For this program, import the CoDrone EDU, time, and random libraries, and then create the drone object and pair to CoDrone EDU. Next, create a for loop that will run three times.<\/p>\n<p>In the loop, create variables that will get a random number between 0 and 255 for red, blue, and green. Those three numbers are combined to change the LED\u2019s color. For example, if your program returned RGB values of 13, 246, and 197, your CoDrone EDU\u2019s LED would turn aqua.<\/p>\n<p>Next, have your drone take off, wait briefly, and then return the red, green, and blue random numbers with a brightness of 100. This should change the color of your drone\u2019s LED. After that, have the drone land.<\/p>\n<p>Try this program out, and once you see what happens, once you get it working, try to add a random value for brightness as well!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\nimport random\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(3):\r\n\r\n    red = random.randint(0,255)\r\n    blue = random.randint(0,255)\r\n    green = random.randint(0,255)\r\n\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n    time.sleep(0.05)\r\n\r\n    drone.set_drone_LED(red,blue,green, 100)\r\n\r\n    drone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af09eac\" name=\"6a2213af09eac\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random Practice Exercises<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To practice our knowledge with the different random functions, try writing programs that do the following:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Print a random value between 100 and 1000<\/li>\n<li>Select a random color from a list of 5 colors of your choosing<\/li>\n<li>Print a random decimal value between 0 and 1<\/li>\n<li>Select a random flight sequence from a list of sequences and have the drone fly the sequence<\/li>\n<li>Select a random color sequence from a list of sequences and have the drone display it<\/li>\n<li>CHALLENGE: Print a random value between 10 and 20 WITHOUT using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">randint(a,b)<\/code> function<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Now try to create a program that selects a random integer for a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move()<\/code>function and a random integer for a flight direction function (pitch, yaw, roll, or throttle). Have the drone take off and fly the direction and duration based on the two random values you generated.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af09f3e\" name=\"6a2213af09f3e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random Flight Path<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this activity, we are going to create a list of flight directions and random numbers and see where our drone goes! To do this, create a new file called <em>random_flight.\u00a0<\/em>Import the\u00a0<em>random<\/em> library.<\/p>\n<p>We are now going to create 3 variables. One is a list of flight directions, the others are the power and duration for that movement:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">directions = [\"yaw\", \"pitch\", \"roll\", \"throttle\"] #different flight path options\r\nduration = random.randint(1,5) # 1 to 5 seconds for our movement\r\npower = random.randint(20,100) # Random power value that starts at 20 so we at least see some movement<\/pre>\n<p>Now that we have these created, let&#8217;s make our movement based off of the random values:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()\r\nrand_dir = random.choice(directions)\r\nif rand_dir == \"yaw\":\r\n    drone.set_yaw(power)\r\nelif rand_dir == \"pitch\":\r\n    drone.set_pitch(power)\r\nelif rand_dir == \"roll\":\r\n    drone.set_roll(power)\r\nelif rand_dir == \"throttle\":\r\n    drone.set_throttle(power)<\/pre>\n<p>Finally, add the move and land commands:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.move(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Try out the program and see how it works! Once you have it running, <em>can you think of any other additions or optimizations you could make to the code?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af0a08e\" name=\"6a2213af0a08e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Number Guessing Program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>You know that game that starts with \u201cI\u2019m thinking of a number\u2026\u201d and you have to guess which number the person is thinking of?<\/em> You\u2019re going to play that with your drone!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing libraries (direction, time, and random), have your drone take off. Next, create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">throttle<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set its value to 20.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">number<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will store and return a number between 1 and 10. Next, create a while loop. Create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">guess<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will prompt the user to input a number between 1 and 10, and will then store that value. Within the while loop, set up conditionals. If their guess is too low, the drone should throttle down. If their guess is too high, the drone should throttle up. If their guess is just right, the drone should do a flip. The program should also include a print statement for when the user guesses a number that is not between 1-10, and a break if they want to quit the program. Finally, the drone should land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\nimport random\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nthrottle = 20\r\n\r\nnumber = random.randint(1, 10)\u00a0 # randomly choose a number between 1-10\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    guess = int(input(\"Guess a number from 1 - 10... or enter 0 to quit: \"))\u00a0 # get input from user\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if guess == 0:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    break\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess &gt; 10 or guess &lt; 0:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Number is not between 1-10. Try again.\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess == number:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Perfect!\")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.flip(\"front\")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 break\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess &lt; number:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Too low!\")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(-throttle)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 elif guess &gt; number:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Too High!\")\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(throttle)\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af0a10a\" name=\"6a2213af0a10a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Random Noises<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, you will have your CoDrone EDU create random noises using the buzzer. To complete this challenge, do the following:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose the random notes that your drone will choose from and place them in a void function that has one parameter, iterations.<\/li>\n<li>Inside the function, the iteration parameter will be used to determine how many times the notes will repeat in a loop.<\/li>\n<li>For each loop iteration, generate a random note and duration and have the CoDrone EDU produce that sound.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Once you have completed this program, your drone should produce random sounds that repeat several times depending on your input.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213af0a163\" name=\"6a2213af0a163\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about the random Python library and how it works! You learned how the library can be used to generate random numbers and how those values can be used in a program.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-5-random-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4732,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2122,2134,2138],"class_list":["post-4402","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-random","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4402","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":32,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4402\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13322,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4402\/revisions\/13322"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4732"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4402"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4402"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4402"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}